cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Cross-Regime Robustness of Volatility Forecasting: Why a
  Two-Parameter Mean-Reversion Anchor Generalizes Where
  XGBoost, Random Forests, and Transformers Fail
message: >-
  If you use this software or the accompanying paper,
  please cite it using these metadata.
type: article
authors:
  - given-names: Kyungmo
    family-names: Doh
    # orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0009-2023-3147
abstract: >-
  An empirical study of out-of-distribution robustness in
  volatility forecasting on the KOSPI200. Six models are
  compared under controlled regime transitions. Data-driven
  models (XGBoost, Random Forest, Transformer) retain strong
  in-regime skill but zero cross-regime skill, failing
  asymmetrically. A two-parameter mean-reversion anchor with
  an adaptive long-run level generalizes across regimes; a
  neural network inherits this robustness only when the
  anchor is injected architecturally, not as a soft penalty.
keywords:
  - cross-regime generalization
  - realized volatility
  - physics-informed neural networks
  - out-of-distribution robustness
  - mean reversion
